{"accessed_at":"2026-11-08T05:08:00.466Z","response":"Antibiotics: Origin, Nature and Properties (https://api.pageplace.de/preview/DT0400.9781483223056_A25802429/preview-9781483223056_A25802429.pdf)\nciteturn19search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 13.1 years ago; Chlororaphin is a green pigment isolated in 1894 by Guignard and Sauva- ... is a phenazine derivative containing a carboxamide group at C-1, which was\n14 \nANTIBIOTICS \nC H L O R O R A P H I N \nChlororaphin is a green pigment isolated in 1894 by Guignard and Sauva-\ngeau from cultures of Pseudomonas \nchlororaphis \nG.S. \nIts antibiotic properties \nwere discovered in 1941 by Mcllwain. \nPseudomonas \nchlororaphis \nwas grown at 25°C in a medium containing aspa-\nragine, glycerol and inorganic salts. After 3-4 days' incubation a green pigment \ncrystallized from the cultures, which was separated mechanically and recrystal-\nlized from acetone. Since chlororaphin is easily oxidized by atmospheric oxygen, \nisolation must be carried out in nitrogen atmosphere. \nChlororaphin, C13H10ON3, melts at 228-230°C; and is insoluble in chloro­\nform, benzene and petroleum ether, sparingly soluble in ethanol, and insoluble \nin aqueous acid and alkaline solutions. It changes in the presence of air into \na yellow pigment, oxychlororaphin, C13H9ON3, m.p. 237°C. Oxychlororaphin \nis a phenazine derivative containing a carboxamide group at C-1, which was \nobtained synthetically by heating nitrobenzene and anthranihc acid at 160°C \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPSEUDOM1ONAS AUREOFACIENS NOV. SPEC. AND ITS PIGMENTS (https://repository.tudelft.nl/file/File_0700877a-931e-411b-b0c3-f612deb5e3b7?preview=1)\nciteturn19search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 17.8 years ago; green ... chlororaphis, is the semiquinoine of phenazine-a- ... (1950) proved io(linin, the pigment of Pseudo- ... the problems involved: Tjoa Hin Soey, E. van\nPSEUDOM1ONAS AUREOFACIENS NOV. SPEC. AND ITS PIGMENTS\nA. J. KLUYVER*\nLaboratory for Mlicr-obiology, Technological Unimersity, Delft, Holland\nReceived for publication March 12, 1956\nIt is well knowni that the genus Pseudomonas\ncomllpirises\nseveral\np)igment-producing\nspecies,\nan(l that in all cases in whiich the constitution\nof these pigments\nlhais\nl)een\nestal)lishedl\nthle-\nhave leen found to\nl)e plhenlzine (lerivatives.\nWrede and Strack (1929) were the first to prove\nthis foI pyocyanine, the pigment produce(l by\nPseudomonas aeruginosa (P. pyocyanea). Pyocv-\nanine is now usually consider(ed to be a reso-\nnance hybrid of the mesomeric forms of n-methyl\n1-hydrioxyphenazine\n(Hillemann,\n1938).\nK6gl\nand Postowsky (1930) established that chloio-\nraphine,\nthe\ngreen\npigiment\nof\nPseudomonas\nchlororaphis, is the semiquinoine of phenazine-a-\ncarboxylic acid amide, while Clemo aind Daglish\n(1950) proved io(linin, the pigment of Pseudo-\nnm,onas iodina, to l)e 1:6-dihydroxyphenazine-di-\nn-oxide.\nIn view of these (latta it seems worthwhile to\nMr. C. J. P. Spruit. They obtained convincing\nevridence that the pigment as first isolated by\nBouman was phenazine-a-carboxy lic acid, and\ntherefore\nclosely related\nto chlororaphine. At\nthat time war interfered with the work, and the\nresults obtained remained unpublished.\n* Deceased May 1956.\nl In this connection the pseudomonad strain C\n50 studied by Chinn (1954), which forms greenish\ncrystals in agar media, also asks for attention.\nIsolation and cultural properties of the organism.\nFor reasons which can be left out of consideration\nhere i\\h. Bouman in 1936 Imade some experiments\nin which kerosene was treated with clay. A liter\nof heat-sterilized kerosene was poured into a 2-L\nErlenmeyer flask and 100 g clay-from the river\nMIaas-was added. The flask was kept in an incu-\nbator at 25 C for three weeks. The clay was resus-\npended in the kerosene from time to time by\nshaking. At the end of the period the kerosene\nwas poured off, and a suspension of the clay in\nsterile water was streaked on an agar plate con-\ntaining 1 per cent peptone and 2 per cent glucose.\nAfter two days, incubation at 30 C several colonies\n406--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTHE JOURNAL OF ANTIBIOTICS, SER. A 15(6): 250-253 (1962) (https://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/article/antibioticsa/15/6/15_250/_pdf/-char/ja)\nciteturn19search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.6 years ago; Primocarcin discovered in our laboratory is a new antitumor antibiotic produced by ... chemical structure of this substance were reported in previous papers 1, 2 , 3>. \nIn this paper, ... ascitic forms of EHRLICH carcinoma and Sarcoma 180, and the toxicity of primocarcin are\n250 \nTHE JOURNAL OF ANTIBIOTICS, SER. A \nNov. 196Z \nSTUDIES ON PRIMOCARCIN. IV \nANTITUMOR ACTIVITY OF PRIMOCARCIN ON SOME OF \nTRANSPLANT ABLE MOUSE TUMORS \nCHITOSHI hAKCRA, TosHIO SEcA, K1YosH1 IsoNo & SAllUR◊ SuzuKI \nAntibiotic Laboratory, The Institute of Ph ysical & Chemical Resaerch, Bunkyo-ku, Tokyo \n(Received for publication July 27, 1962) \nPrimocarcin discovered in our laboratory is a new antitumor antibiotic produced by \nNocardia fukayae. \nThe taxonomic studies, \nthe physico-chemical properties and the \nchemical structure of this substance were reported in previous papers 1, 2 , 3>. \nIn this paper, \nthe antitumor effect of primocarcin against transplantable mouse tumors, particularly \nascitic forms of EHRLICH carcinoma and Sarcoma 180, and the toxicity of primocarcin are \npresented. \nExperimentals \nNormal male mice of dd strain, 5 weeks of age and weighing about 20 g at the time \nof the first treatment were used throughout the experiments. \nThe tumors used in the \npresent study are EHRLICH ascites carcinoma and Sarcoma 180 (ascitic). \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n77 \nVol. XV No. 2 \nTHE JOURNAL OF ANTIBIOTICS, SER (https://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/article/antibioticsa/15/2/15_77/_pdf)\nciteturn19search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; carcinoma, a new antibiotic primocarcin was isolated in crystalline form from the culture ... This paper ... * The outline of this work has presented as a short communication, a new antibiotic. primocarcin,\n77 \nVol. XV No. 2 \nTHE JOURNAL OF ANTIBIOTICS, SER. A \nSTUDIES ON PRIMOCARCIN. II \nISOLATION AND PROPERTIES OF PRIMOCARCIN* \nK1Yosm IsoNo & SABURO SuzuKr \nAntibiotic Research Laboratory, The Institute of Physical \n& Chemical Research. Bunkyo-ku. Tokyo \n(Received for publication June 14, 1961) \nIn the course of our antitumor antibiotic screening program using EHRLICH ascites \ncarcinoma, a new antibiotic primocarcin was isolated in crystalline form from the culture \nfiltrate of an actinomycete designated as Nocardia fukayae 1>. The antibiotic is a water-\nsoluble neutral compound having the formula C 8H, 2N 20 3 and shows marked tumor-inhibitory \neffects against ascites types of EHRLICH carzinoma and CROCKER sarcoma 180. \nThis paper \ndeals with ferme:1tation and isolation procedures as well as the chemical and biological \nproperties of the antibiotic. \nFermentation of Nocardia fukayae \nA culture of Nocardia .fukayae on BENNET agar slant was used for inoculation to 70 ml \nof the medium in 300-ml ERLENMEYER flask, which was consisted of 2 % glucose, 1 % soluble \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nVOL. XXXIV NO. 8 — THE JOURNAL OF ANTIBIOTICS — 1073 (https://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/article/antibiotics1968/34/8/34_8_1073/_pdf)\nciteturn19search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 20.7 years ago; Crawled: 19.0 years ago; A new neutral macrolide antibiotic named cytovaricin has been discovered in our screening program using Yoshida sarcoma cells in tissue culture. ... We wish to describe here the isolation and characterization of cytovaricin from cultures of *Streptomyces* sp.\nVOL. XXXIV NO. 8 — THE JOURNAL OF ANTIBIOTICS — 1073\n\n# CYTOVARICIN, A NOVEL ANTIBIOTIC\n\nSir:\n\nA new neutral macrolide antibiotic named cytovaricin has been discovered in our screening program using Yoshida sarcoma cells in tissue culture. The absolute structure (Fig. 1) was determined by X-ray analysis and the isolation of D-cymarose by acid hydrolysis, as will be reported separately. We wish to describe here the isolation and characterization of cytovaricin from cultures of *Streptomyces* sp. No. H-230 which resembles *Streptomyces diastatochromogenes*. This strain was isolated from a soil sample collected in Yanai-shi, Yamaguchi Prefecture, Japan.\n\nThe strain was cultured at 27°C for 72 hours in jar fermentors containing 18 liters of a medium which was composed of 2% glucose, 1% soluble starch, 0.1% meat extract, 0.4% dry yeast, 2.5% soybean flour, 0.2% NaCl and 0.005% K₂HPO₄. The fermentation broth (36 liters) was filtered and the filtrate (pH 7.6) was extracted twice with ethyl acetate (15 and 10 liters). --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStudies on primocarcin. II. Isolation and properties of primocarcin (https://inference.eurekamag.com/research/025/566/025566003.php)\nciteturn19search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 64.9 years ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; The chemical structure of primocarcin Jour Antibiot Ser A tokyo 15(2): 80-87 ... 1960: A new antitumor antibiotic, primocarcin Jour Antibiotics: 416\n\n# Studies on primocarcin. II. Isolation and properties of primocarcin\n\n##### Isono, Kiyoshi; Suzuki, S.\n\n#### Jour Antibiot Ser A tokyo 15(2): 77-79\n\n#### 1962\n\nAccession: 025566003\n\n##### Summary\n\nA new tumor-inhibitory antibiotic, primocarcin, was isolated in crystalline form from a culture filtrate of Nocardia fukayae. It is a neutral compound with the formula C8H12NO3 and showed inhibitory effect against the experimental tumors of mice.\n\n#### References\n\nItakura, Chitoshi; Sega, Toshio; Isono, Kitoshi; Suzuki, S. 1962: Studies on primocarcin. IV. Antitumor activity of primocarcin on some transplantable mouse tumors Jour Antibiot tokyo Ser A 15(6): 250-253\n\n* * *\n\nIsono, K. 1962: Studies on primocarcin. ID. The chemical structure of primocarcin Jour Antibiot Ser A tokyo 15(2): 80-87\n\n* * *\n\nIsona, K. 1961: The structure of primocarcin Jour Antibiot Tokyo 14a(3): 160\n\n* * *\n\nBowman, R.E.; Closier, M.D.; Islip, P.J. 1964: The synthesis of primocarcin Tetrahedron Letters 28: 1897-1898\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Journal of Antibiotics, Series A (https://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/browse/antibioticsa/15/2/_contents/-char/en)\nciteturn19search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 64.9 years ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; This paper feals with taxonimic studies of the primocarcin-producing organism, Nocardia sp. ... In the course of our antitumor antibiotic screen program using Ehrlich ascites carcinoma, a new antibiotic primocarcin was isolated in crystalline from the culture filtrate of an actinomycete designated as Nocardia fukayae^{1)}.\nThese derivatives except the promo derivative (III) showed significant lowering of toxicity. This is probably due to the fact that the promine atom generally fails to form hydrogen bond.\n\nView full abstract\n\nDownload PDF (730K)\n\n  * Studies on Primocarcin. I Taxonomic Studies on the Primocarcin-Producing Organism Nocardia Fukatae\n\nJunsaku Nagatsu, Kiyoshi Isono, Saburō Suzuki\n\n1962Volume 15Issue 2 Pages 75-76\nPublished: 1962\nReleased on J-STAGE: July 07, 2020\n\nDOIhttps://doi.org/10.11554/antibioticsa.15.2_75\n\nJOURNAL FREE ACCESS\n\nShow abstract Hide abstract\n\nFor the purpose of searching for antitumor antibiotic, we isolated Actinomycetes from the soil of Japan, and, as the result of screening the broth filtrates of Actinomycetes inhibiting Ehrlich ascites tumor, we discovered a new antibiotic, primocarcin. This paper feals with taxonimic studies of the primocarcin-producing organism, Nocardia sp. II-41 which is named Nocardia fukayae.\n\nView full abstract\n\nDownload PDF (639K)\n\n  * Studies on Primocarcin. II Isolation and Properties of Primocarcin\n\nKiyoshi Isono, Saburō Suzuki\n\n1962Volume 15Issue 2 Pages 77-79\nPublished: 1962\nReleased on J-STAGE: July 07, 2020\n\nDOIhttps://doi.org/10.11554/antibioticsa.15.2_77\n\nJOURNAL FREE ACCESS\n\nShow abstract Hide abstract\n\nIn the course of our antitumor antibiotic screen program using Ehrlich ascites carcinoma, a new antibiotic primocarcin was isolated in crystalline from the culture filtrate of an actinomycete designated as Nocardia fukayae^{1)}. The antibiotic is a water-soluble neutral compound having the formula C_{8}H_{12}N_{2}O_{3} and shows marked tumor-inhibitory effects against ascites type of Ehrlich carzinoma and Crocker sarcoma 180. This paper deals with fermentation and isolation procefures as well as the chemical and biological properties of the antibiotic.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n73. The synthesis of primocarcin and analogues (https://pubs.rsc.org/en/content/articlelanding/1965/jr/jr9650000470)\nciteturn19search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 months ago; The synthesis of primocarcin and analogues ...     Paper\n\n## 73. The synthesis of primocarcin and analogues\n\nR. E. Bowman,^{}   M. D. Closier^{}  and  P. J. Islip^{}  \n\n### Abstract\n\nThe first page of this article is displayed as the abstract.\n\nImage: 73. The synthesis of primocarcin and analogues\n\nYou have access to this article\n\n## Article information\n\nDOI\n    https://doi.org/10.1039/JR9650000470\n\nArticle type\n    Paper\n\n### Download Citation\n\nJ. Chem. Soc., 1965, 470-473\n\n[Select] [Input]\n\n### Permissions\n\nRequest permissions\n\n### 73. The synthesis of primocarcin and analogues\n\nR. E. Bowman, M. D. Closier and P. J. Islip, J. Chem. Soc., 1965, 470 DOI: 10.1039/JR9650000470---------------------------------------------------------------